   "id": "idea-097",
   "title": "REEL Skript — Steve Jobs Buch-Geschichte für Filmmaker (Way to Oscar Episode 1)",
   "rawIdea": "REEL — Steve Jobs: eine Geschichte für Filmmaker\n\nBlueprint: Personal Story × Motivational Montage\nLänge: ca 85 Sek\nSprache: Englisch\nKernpunkt: Die stärksten Ideen kommen aus dem Bauch. Komitees polieren sie flach. Genau das was unvernünftig wirkt, macht den Film zu deinem.\n\n---\n\nTEXT-HOOK:\n1. 'a steve jobs story every filmmaker needs' (Pick)\n2. 'the $60 decision that built apple'\n3. 'why your gut beats the boardroom'\n\n---\n\nVOICEOVER:\n\nTake 1: There's a story in the Steve Jobs book that every filmmaker should hear.\n\nTake 2: Jony Ive walks into Steve's office with the new iMac. Translucent plastic. Five colors. Sixty dollars per unit, three times more than a normal case.\n\nTake 3: At any other company, that idea would have died in the next meeting.\n\nTake 4: First a market study. Then a focus group. Then a committee of twenty people, all wanting it a little safer, a little cheaper, a little more like everyone else. Until the original feeling is gone.\n\nTake 5: Steve looked at it and said yes.\n\nTake 6: Because he understood something the committees never see. They think color is decoration. Steve knew it was the whole experience. The reason a kid wants this one in their room. The reason a first-time buyer trusts the machine. The expensive part wasn't a cost. It was the product.\n\nTake 7: Here's what that means for your films.\n\nTake 8: Your strongest ideas come from a feeling. Not from analysis. The moment you run them through too many opinions, the one thing only you could have made disappears.\n\nTake 9: The shot you're afraid is too much is probably the one that makes the film yours. Don't smooth it out. Don't ask the committee. That edge is the whole point.\n\nTake 10: Trust the feeling. Keep building. Let's get better together.\n\n---\n\nCAPTION:\n\na steve jobs story every filmmaker needs.\n\nthe $60 case cost three times more.\nno studies. no committees. just a yes.\n\nthe expensive part wasn't a cost.\nit was the product.\n\ntrust the feeling.\nkeep the edge.\n\n---\n\nB-ROLL:\n- Buch in der Hand, Cover-Shot\n- Markierte Stelle Close-up\n- Bondi Blue iMac, alle fünf Farben wenn möglich (Stock)\n- Komitee-Visual: Hände am Whiteboard, Post-Its, Meeting-Raum (Stock)\n- DaVinci-Timeline, ein einzelner riskanter Cut, Hand auf Maus\n- Hand-auf-Brust-Geste\n- Walking-Shot mit Kamera, allein\n- Outro: Buch und Notizbuch nebeneinander, neuer Buchrücken daneben (Teaser Episode 2)\n\n---\n\nBELEG AUS DEM BUCH:\nJony Ive: 'In most places that decision would have taken months. Steve did it in a half hour.'\nErgebnis: 800.000 verkaufte iMacs im ersten Jahr, 32% Erstkäufer, schnellst-verkaufter Computer in Apples Geschichte bis dahin.",
   "category": "⚡ Direkt umsetzbar",
   "spark": "Eine konkrete Geschichte aus dem Steve-Jobs-Buch übersetzt für Filmmaker. Die teure iMac-Entscheidung als Trojanisches Pferd für eine grössere Lesson: das Teure war das Magische. Komitees glätten Ideen flach. Genau die Kante macht den Film zu deinem.",
   "pattern": "Erste Episode der Way-to-Oscar-Buch-Serie auf YouTube. Format: ein Buch — eine Geschichte — eine Lesson für Filmmaker. Knüpft an die bestehende McKee-Reihe an. Verwandt mit Notion-Eintrag 'Iteration in der Öffentlichkeit' und den zwei vorigen Steve-Jobs-Einträgen.",
   "nextStep": "1) Reel aufnehmen — 11labs Voice (Grandpa Spuds oder Adam-Slow). 2) B-Roll sammeln: Buch in der Hand, Bondi Blue iMac (Stock), DaVinci-Timeline-Cut, Walking-Shot. 3) Posten als Episode 1 der Buch-Serie. 4) Performance nach 48h checken — wenn zieht: zweites Buch direkt planen. 5) Learnings zurück in den Reel-Skript-Skill.",
